2186045417] [17592186120633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120634 #edb/ref 1061 16231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7d6d5a0-3ce7-530f-a0b1-24773b682d6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120635 #edb/ref 1061 16232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eeaa9b6a-90e5-5a18-85ae-913e7cbc0f24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121022 #edb/ref 1064 "In this topic, we will consider a few more properties of determinants. Let's start with the following important property. \n\n*The determinant of the product of two square matrices is equal to the product of the determinants.*\n\nSymbolically, if ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](images/25/256531b7f0ddb14dd11a59a3019a3361f1607d3fe16fa6d530a461344de8f39e.svg) are both square matrices, then\n\n![](images/e5/e59b47ca5462a066093945e2698404e595a92e9e70a2b8c4f8b2f30df9c9fbf1.svg)\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to find the determinant of the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) that is defined as follows:\n![](images/9a/9a705ea4bd482cdf83251288108b48ff5bc7354c9d51c1b710b9e734c8a32dea.svg)\n\nTo easily compute ![](images/da/dab9a65c1cba3cd9f271a1910b060e5428813107b3b54945776aab3839003d45.svg), we first compute the determinants of the two matrices and then multiply the results. Also, notice that both matrices in our product are triangular, so their determinants are given by the product of the diagonal entries:\n\n![](images/fa/fa4214541c12b0a761fd7cb17f4efc2f6b4be2c1424094483a3c2f61ab63c0a0.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121022 #edb/ref 1065 3009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121022 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a8abcbc-c41d-5553-8b2e-daa7256d7d34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121022 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121023 #edb/ref 1064 "Another useful property of determinants is that, for any invertible matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the above property is true, remember that $A \\cdot A^{-1} = I$ for any invertible matrix $A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =I \\\\ det(A\\cdot {A}^{-1}) & =det(I) \\\\ det(A)\\cdot det({A}^{-1}) & =1 \\\\ det({A}^{-1}) & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** We can only apply the formula $\\det(A^{-1}) = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}$ if $A$ is invertible, meaning that $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121023 #edb/ref 1065 3010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121023 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "043b8a4a-8f67-5ae8-a7ae-bbda3fa2bdbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121023 #edb/ref 1067 "The Determinant of an Inverse Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121024 #edb/ref 1064 "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\,\\,=det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121024 #edb/ref 1065 3011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121024 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f26cf5f4-fd6d-5502-94d6-4b34bd2568ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121024 #edb/ref 1067 "The Linearity of the Determinant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352358 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59a4042a-f605-574f-a228-ff4ae6d308e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352581 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352581 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352581 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "73d447eb-1e81-51dc-b291-5725cee52d82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352586 #edb/ref 1080 "-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352586 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352586 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353093 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353093 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353093 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b27eb1ec-b060-5c68-b2a6-225457f0de0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353125 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353125 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353125 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b5c62a94-26b3-5bea-ae7b-f2fbef1384ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186352586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353991 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "607dd21d-aeb7-5920-91bc-f439f202ce2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354637 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354637 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354637 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354637 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354637 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354637 #edb/ref 1074 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1774->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050765 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
